ef 17592186388467 13194139536197 true] [17592186388463 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388470 13194139536197 true] [17592186388463 1089 "q-338082" 13194139536197 true] [17592186388463 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536197 true] [17592186388463 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2y=x$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-(x+2)y+5x=0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139536197 true] [17592186388463 1113 #uuid "e8b12875-ae8a-5464-b1d4-8be3ec87c5f9" 13194139536197 true] [17592186388463 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-(x+2)y+5x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-(x+2)y=-5x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-5x,$ which is different from zero." 13194139536197 true] [17592186388464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388465 13194139536197 true] [17592186388464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388466 13194139536197 true] [17592186388464 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139536197 true] [17592186388464 1041 "field-1" 13194139536197 true] [17592186388464 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388466 13194139536197 true] [17592186388464 1110 #uuid "eb792e9f-5a42-5809-8b49-600b3cb18c2d" 13194139536197 true] [17592186388465 1034 "homogeneous" 13194139536197 true]